# Upstream's own ignore conventions, kept verbatim. Nothing above this line is
# ours, and nothing below it removes an upstream entry to make room for a
# fork-owned one.
#
# The OSGeo/PROJ 9.9.0 release tarball ships no `.gitignore`: `ls -a` on the
# unpacked `proj-9.9.0/` shows `AUTHORS.md CITATION CITATION.cff CMakeLists.txt
# COPYING ChangeLog INSTALL NEWS.md README.md cmake data examples include man
# scripts src test` and no dotfile, and `UPSTREAM.sha256` records exactly the
# ten top-level files plus the eight directories with no `.gitignore` among them.
# So there is no upstream ignore file to preserve or merge with, and every entry
# below is this fork's own. The entries in the first block are upstream's *build
# system* outputs rather than an upstream ignore file, listed so that a
# CMake-produced object, archive or executable cannot be committed into a tree
# that is built with Zig instead: `src/lib_proj.cmake` and the `bin_*.cmake`
# includes under `src/apps/` produce exactly those kinds of files.
#
#   * `CMakeFiles/`, `CMakeCache.txt`, `cmake_install.cmake`,
#     `Makefile`, `CTestTestfile.cmake` and `CMakeFiles/` come from configuring
#     or building PROJ with CMake (`CMakeLists.txt` has no out-of-source guard for
#     every artefact; the generated parser-refresh targets
#     `generate_wkt1_parser`/`generate_wkt2_parser` at
#     `src/lib_proj.cmake:326-368` write `wkt1_generated_parser.c` and
#     `wkt2_generated_parser.c` back into `src/` as well).
#   * `CTestTestfile.cmake` and `Testing/` come from a `ctest` run.
#   * `proj.db`, `all.sql.in`, `PROJ_DB_SQL_MD5.h` and the `o/` and
#     `for_tests/` trees are the outputs of `data/CMakeLists.txt:39-49` and of
#     `generate_proj_db.cmake`. **This package generates all four into Zig's
#     cache instead**, through the host-run generator in `build.zig`; they are
#     listed because a `cmake` run in this tree would write them into `data/`,
#     and the generated `proj.db` must never be committed as though it were
#     upstream material.
#   * `*.gtx` and `*.gsb` are grid files. **None of them is upstream
#     material**: `data/` ships no grid (`data/CH:9` says the grids must be
#     downloaded separately and names `CHENyx06_ETRS.gsb`), and `data/tests/`
#     holds only the test grids upstream's own `data/CMakeLists.txt:76-92`
#     copies, which are recorded in `UPSTREAM.sha256` as imported files. The
#     two patterns below are the only broad entries that could ever match an
#     imported grid, which is why the imported ones are named explicitly in
#     `UPSTREAM.sha256` and why `tools/check-provenance.sh` reports a missing
#     file rather than a reset Git index: an ignored imported file is reported,
#     never silently accepted. `*.tif` is deliberately *not* ignored: `data/`
#     installs three `*.schema.json` files that name GeoTIFF in their own
#     descriptions, `data/sql/grid_transformation.sql` refers to `.tif` grids in
#     its own text, and a future upstream test grid could be a GeoTIFF. Nothing
#     in this package downloads a grid, so there is nothing for `*.tif` to
#     catch.
CMakeFiles/
CMakeCache.txt
cmake_install.cmake
CTestTestfile.cmake
Testing/
CMakeScripts/
Makefile
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.so
*.so.*
*.lib
*.exp
*.pdb
*.dll
*.exe
*.manifest
*.dmp
*.swp

# Generated datum database and its inputs, if a CMake build ever runs here.
data/proj.db
data/all.sql.in
data/PROJ_DB_SQL_MD5.h
data/all.sql.in.extra_validation
data/proj.db.extra_validation
data/for_tests/
data/o/
data/file_embed/
*.gtx
*.gsb

# zig build artefacts (fork addition).
#
# These are directories rather than single files because a normal `zig build`
# creates them, and a normal `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into the
# package published from it. None of them is named in build.zig.zon `.paths`, and
# that list is an allowlist, so an ignored directory cannot become part of the
# package even if this file were deleted.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/
# Zig 0.16 fetched dependencies here; not rechecked with Zig 0.17.0.
# Keep the path ignored for future dependencies.
zig-pkg/

# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. It is ignored here as well as in consumer/.gitignore so a build
# run from the repository root cannot commit it by accident.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts (fork addition).
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild)
# into the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved inputs
# and its state beside the project. None of them is named in build.zig.zon
# `.paths`, so an ignored path cannot become part of the package either.
# `devenv.lock` and `flake.lock` are deliberately *not* here: they are real
# inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be the wrong way to
# keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/